ey get (2,2) next. So I need to block that. But since it's my turn, I can place in (2,2) to block that diagonal. Alternatively, if they try to make a vertical line, they have X in the center. If they place in (0,1) or (2,1), that would be a vertical line. But I don't know what they'll do next.\n\nWait, but in the current state, the other player's X is in (1,1). So if they place in (0,1) or (2,1), that would be a vertical line. But since it's my turn, I can block that by placing in (1,0) or (1,2) to block the vertical? Wait, no. If they place in (0,1), then the vertical line would be (0,1), (1,1), (2,1). So if I place in (2,1), that would block that line. But in the current state, (2,1) is empty. Alternatively, if they place in (1,0), then the vertical line would be (1,0), (1,1), (1,2). So if I place in (1,2) or (1,0), that would block that line. But the other player's next move is not known. Since it's my turn, I need to choose a move that is the best possible to block any immediate threats.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to take the center, but that's already taken. So maybe I should take a corner to block a diagonal. For example, if I place in (0,0), that would block the diagonal from (0,0) to (1,1) to (2,2). Similarly, placing in (2,2) would block that diagonal. So perhaps that's a good move.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should take the middle of the top row (0,1) to block any horizontal lines. But the other player's X is in the center, so if they place in (0,1), that would be a horizontal line with their X in the center? No, that's not possible. Wait, the horizontal line would be row 0: (0,0), (0,1), (0,2). The other player has X in (1,1). So if they place in (0,1), that's not part of a horizontal line. Wait, maybe I'm getting confused.\n\nWait, the other player's X is in (1,1). If they place in (0,1), then they have X in (0,1) and (1,1). But that's only two in a row. So they need a third. So maybe that's not a threat.", "episode_score": -11.0, "llm_raw_t