ing my own lines.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to block the center. Wait, but the center is already X. So perhaps the opponent is trying to make a diagonal. For example, if they place X in (0,0) and (2,2), then they can win. But since they have X in the center, maybe they can complete a diagonal. But in the current state, the X is in the center. So if they place in (0,0) and (2,2), that's a diagonal. But since it's my turn, I need to block that. Wait, but the opponent is X, and I'm O. So if I place an O in (0,0) or (2,2), that would block their potential diagonal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the opponent is trying to make a vertical line. For example, if they place in (0,1), (1,1), (2,1), but that's already (1,1) is X. So if they place in (0,1) and (2,1), that's a vertical line. But I need to block that. So if I place in (0,1) or (2,1), that would block them. But wait, the legal actions for me are all except (1,1). So the available positions are all cells except (1,1). So I can choose any of the other 8 cells.\n\nBut the opponent's next move (if they have a chance) would be to complete a line. So I need to block their possible next moves. Let me think: the opponent has X in the center. If they place another X in (0,1), then they have a vertical line (if they can get (2,1) next). But if I place O in (0,1), that would block that. Alternatively, if they place in (1,0), then they can have a vertical line if they get (1,2). So maybe I should block that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best strategy is to take a corner. Let's say I take (0,0). Then, if the opponent tries to take (0,1), I can block. Alternatively, maybe taking the center is not possible, but maybe the best move is to take a corner. Let me think about possible moves.\n\nAnother approach: the opponent has the center. So they can win in a few moves if they can get two more in a line. So I need to block any possible lines.<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your a