ck middle row.\n- (0,1) or (2,1) to block middle column.\n- (0,0) or (2,2) to block main diagonal.\n- (0,2) or (2,0) to block the other diagonal.\n\nBut since I'm O, I need to choose one of these. Which is the best move?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should look for opportunities to create my own lines. For example, if I can create two in a row, column, or diagonal, that would be good.\n\nLooking at the current board, there are no O's yet. So I need to place my O in a way that can set up a future win.\n\nBut since X is the one who just played in the center, maybe the best strategy is to block X's possible threats first.\n\nSo, let's consider the possible moves. Let's check which of the possible moves would block the most immediate threats.\n\nFor example, if I place O in (0,1), that would block the middle column. Similarly, placing O in (0,0) would block the main diagonal. But maybe the best is to block the middle column, since X could complete that column in the next move.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best is to take a corner to start building a line. For example, if I take (0,0), that's a corner. Then, if X doesn't take the opposite corner, I can<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_'