r. So the other player's X is in (1,1). So, if they place in (1,0) and (1,2), they could complete the second row. But they have only one X now. So maybe they are trying to get two more in a row, column, or diagonal.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the other player is trying to form a diagonal. For example, if they place in (0,0) and (2,2), they could win diagonally. But they only have one X now. So I need to block that.\n\nSo, the possible moves for me are to block any potential threats. Let's see: if I place in (0,0), that would block the diagonal from (0,0) to (2,2) if the other player places in (2,2) next. But maybe that's not the best move. Alternatively, if I place in (0,1), that would block the first row if they place in (0,2) and (0,0). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should try to create a position where I can win. For example, if I place in (0,0), then (0,2), then (0,1) would be a row. But that's three moves. But since it's my turn, I need to think about immediate moves.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should place in the center, but it's already taken. So I need to choose from the available positions.\n\nAnother approach: look for the best move that maximizes my chances. Since the other player just placed in the center, maybe I should take a corner. Let's say I take (0,0). Then, I can start building a diagonal. Alternatively, take (0,2), same thing. Or (2,0) or (2,2). Let's pick (0,0). That would be O(0,0). Then, the board would have O in (0,0), and the other player's X in (1,1). Then, if they try to complete the diagonal, they need (2,2). But I can block that by placing in (2,2) if needed.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should block the other player's possible<|im_end|>"}, {"prompt": "system\nYou are an AI agent that makes optimal decisions to win in the game of tic-tac-toe.user\nGAME RULES:\n1. Tic-tac-toe is a two-player board game played on a three-by-three grid. The grid is 0-indexed, where (0,0) is the top-left corner and (2,2) is the bottom-right corner.\n2. Two players take turns placing their marks X and O in empty cells of the grid.\n3. The player who first places three of their marks in a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line wins.\n4. If all cells are filled and no player wins, the game ends in a draw.\n\nPLAYER INFORMATION:\n1. Your mark is O. You are competing with another player controlling the mark X.\n2. In each of your turns:\n   a. The game state demonstrates the current board with a three-line text grid, where 'X' and 'O' are the marks of the two players, and '_' represents empty cells.\n   b. You need to chose an action to place your mark in an empty cell, based on the given game state and the history of your decisions.\n   c. All legal actions for the current turn are provided in the format of `<O({row},{column})>`, where `O` is your mark, and {row} and {column} are integers indicating the row and column of the cell to place your mark.\n\nRESPONSE INSTRUCTIONS:\nAlways choose only one action from the legal actions and output `<answer>{your chosen action}</answer>` with no extra text after you finish the thinking process. For example, `<answer><O(0,0)></answer>`. Strictly follow the above format and keep your thinking process concise. Responses that do not follow the format will result in immediate loss of the game.\n\nInformation of Turn-1:\n\nThis is the other player's turn. The game state, legal actions, as well as the chosen action of the other player for this turn are provided below.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n___\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<X(0,0)>, <X(0,1)>, <X(0,2)>, <X(1,0)>, <X(1,1)>, <X(1,2)>, <X(2,0)>, <X(2,1)>, <X(2,2)>.\n\nCHOSEN ACTION:\n<X(1,1)>\nInformation of Turn-2:\n\nThis is your turn. The game state and legal actions for this turn are provided below. Please choose your action and strictly follow the given output format in the response instructions.\n\nGAME STATE:\n___\n_X_\n___\n\nLEGAL ACTIONS:\n<O(0,0)>, <O(0,1)>, <O(0,2)>, <O(1,0)>, <O(1,2)>, <O(2,0)>, <O(2,1)>, <O(2,2)>.\n\nassistant\n", "response"